<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reign of Terror</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1793">1793</a>–<a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as The Terror</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Reign of Terror was the period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, from September 1793 to July 1794, in which the revolutionary government used mass arrests and executions against those it considered enemies of the Revolution. Directed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-of-public-safety">Committee of Public Safety</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilien-robespierre">Maximilien Robespierre</a> was the leading voice, and enforced by revolutionary tribunals, it sent some 17,000 people to their deaths by official sentence, while many more died in prison or in the suppression of revolts such as that in the Vendée. It ended with the fall of Robespierre on 9 Thermidor (27 July 1794).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reign-of-terror.jpg" alt="Reign of Terror" width="330" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exécution_de_Marie_Antoinette_le_16_octobre_1793.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Historians disagree about when the Terror began. Some give 10 March 1793, when the Revolutionary Tribunal was created, or 5 September 1793; others point to the September Massacres of 1792. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had declared war on 20 April 1792, and after the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> the First Coalition, made up of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Holland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, attacked from all sides. Albert Soboul wrote that terror, at first an improvised response to defeat, became an instrument of victory once it was organised.</p><p>Within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the Federalist revolts and the rising in the Vendée, which began in March 1793 in response to mass conscription, added to the sense of emergency. The urban workers known as sans-culottes pressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> for harsh measures against opponents. Robespierre held that a republic needed virtue and that those who resisted it had to be removed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 6 April 1793 the Convention established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-of-public-safety">Committee of Public Safety</a>, which gradually became the wartime government. On 2 June the sans-culottes surrounded the Convention, which arrested 29 Girondist leaders. Robespierre joined the Committee on 27 July. On 5 September Bertrand Barère proposed that terror be made the order of the day. The Law of Suspects of 17 September authorised the imprisonment of vaguely defined suspects and overfilled the prisons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a> was executed on 16 October and the Girondins on 31 October.</p><p>In the provinces, representatives on mission put down revolts: Joseph Fouché and Jean-Marie Collot d'Herbois at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, Jean-Baptiste Carrier at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>, where he ordered drownings. In March 1794 the Hébertists were executed, and on 5 April the Dantonists, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-danton">Georges Danton</a>, who had called for clemency. The Law of 22 Prairial, passed on 10 June, simplified procedure; the period that followed is called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Terror</a>, and 1,366 people were executed between 10 June and 27 July.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The French victory at Fleurus on 26 June 1794 undermined the case for wartime measures. Robespierre was overthrown by men who wanted a more radical policy together with moderates who opposed the revolutionary government; the Law of 22 Prairial was one of the charges against him. He was arrested, wounded in the jaw in circumstances that remain unclear, and guillotined the next day with Saint-Just and Georges Couthon.</p><p>At least 300,000 suspects were arrested; some 17,000 were officially executed, and perhaps 10,000 more died in prison or without trial. Jean-Clément Martin argues that the Convention never instituted a system of terror as such, while Albert Mathiez held that terror was a necessary reaction to circumstances.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Reign of Terror” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The period that followed saw the establishment of the National Convention and the <b>Reign of Terror</b>, in which perceived enemies of the Republic were executed by guillotine at an ever-increasing rate, then the Thermidorian Reaction and the founding of the Directory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-first-republic">French First Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Reign of Terror</b> of 1793–1794, the Napoleonic Wars and the restoration of monarchies at the Congress of Vienna in 1815 are usually taken to close the age.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Oliver Cromwell’s Protectorate of 1653–1658 was described as a military dictatorship by his enemies and by some modern scholars, and Maximilien Robespierre was called a dictator while the Committee of Public Safety directed the <b>Reign of Terror</b> in France in 1793–1794.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Dietrich himself was executed the next year, during the <b>Reign of Terror</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a></li></ul></section>
